1
00:03:44,516 --> 00:03:50,516
M*A*S*H Gives A D*A*M*N.

2
00:07:29,032 --> 00:07:35,032
M*A*S*H is a comedy, drama, war film directed by Robert Altman.

3
00:11:13,548 --> 00:11:19,548
The staff of a Korean War field hospital use humor and hijinks to
keep their sanity in the face of the horror of war.

4
00:14:58,064 --> 00:15:04,064
Hawkeye Pierce, played by Donald Sutherland.

5
00:18:42,580 --> 00:18:48,580
Trapper John McIntyre, played by Elliott Gould.

6
00:22:27,096 --> 00:22:33,096
Duke Forrest, played by Tom Skerritt.

7
00:26:11,612 --> 00:26:17,612
Maj. Margaret 'Hot Lips' O'Houlihan, played by Sally Kellerman.

8
00:29:56,129 --> 00:30:02,129
Maj. Frank Burns, played by Robert Duvall.

9
00:33:40,645 --> 00:33:46,645
Lt. Col. Henry Blake, played by Roger Bowen.

10
00:37:25,161 --> 00:37:31,161
Marrakesh or Marrakech (; Arabic: مراكش, romanized: murrākuš,
pronounced [murraːkuʃ]) is the fourth-largest city in Morocco.

11
00:41:09,677 --> 00:41:15,677
It is one of the four imperial cities of Morocco and is
the capital of the Marrakesh-Safi region.

12
00:44:54,193 --> 00:45:00,193
The city lies west of the foothills of the Atlas Mountains.

13
00:48:38,709 --> 00:48:44,709
The city was founded circa 1070 by Abu Bakr ibn Umar as
the capital of the Almoravid dynasty.

14
00:52:23,225 --> 00:52:29,225
The Almoravids established the first major structures in the
city and shaped its layout for centuries to come.

15
00:56:07,741 --> 00:56:13,741
The red walls of the city, built by Ali ibn Yusuf in 1122–1123, and various buildings constructed in red
sandstone afterwards, have given the city the nickname of the "Red City" or "Ochre City".

16
00:59:52,258 --> 00:59:58,258
Marrakesh grew rapidly and established itself as a cultural,
religious, and trading centre for the Maghreb.

17
01:03:36,774 --> 01:03:42,774
After a period of decline, Marrakesh regained its status in the early 16th century as the capital of the Saadian dynasty,
with sultans Abdallah al-Ghalib and Ahmad al-Mansur embellishing the city with an array of sumptuous monuments.

18
01:07:21,290 --> 01:07:27,290
Beginning in the 17th century, the city became popular among Sufi
pilgrims for its seven patron saints who are buried here.

19
01:11:05,806 --> 01:11:11,806
In 1912, the French Protectorate in Morocco was established and T'hami El Glaoui became Pasha of Marrakesh and generally
held this position until the independence of Morocco and the reestablishment of the monarchy in 1956.

20
01:14:50,322 --> 01:14:56,322
Marrakesh comprises an old fortified city packed with vendors and their stalls.

21
01:18:34,838 --> 01:18:40,838
This medina quarter is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and contains the Jemaa el-Fnaa square, a large
number of souks (markets), the Kutubiyya Mosque, and many other historic and cultural sites.

22
01:22:19,354 --> 01:22:25,354
The city serves as a major economic centre and tourist destination.

23
01:26:03,870 --> 01:26:09,870
Property and hotel development in Marrakesh have
grown dramatically in the 21st century.

24
01:29:48,387 --> 01:29:54,387
Marrakesh is particularly popular with the French, and numerous
French celebrities own property in the city.

25
01:33:32,903 --> 01:33:38,903
Marrakesh is served by Marrakesh Menara Airport and by Marrakesh railway
station, which connects the city to Casablanca and northern Morocco.

26
01:37:17,419 --> 01:37:23,419
Marrakesh has several universities and schools, including Cadi Ayyad University.

27
01:41:01,935 --> 01:41:07,935
A number of Moroccan football clubs are based here, including Najm de Marrakech, KAC
Marrakech, Mouloudia de Marrakech and Chez Ali Club de Marrakech.

28
01:44:46,451 --> 01:44:52,451
The Marrakesh Street Circuit hosts the World Touring Car
Championship, Auto GP and FIA Formula Two Championship races.

29
01:48:30,967 --> 01:48:36,967
The film explores themes of american football, dark comedy,
surgeon, american  way of life, korean war (1950-53).

30
01:52:15,483 --> 01:52:21,483
The tone is lighthearted and dark and emotional.
